## Rules

1. Bands are measured on the bounding extent of every 12mm-inset legal magnet-centre segment. Not
   outer-outline boxes.
2. Band and frame come from the SAME box and therefore cannot disagree. (This is what deleting the
   mass-depth dial bought; under the old two-depth model they were two different readings.)
3. The initial midpoint is a probe, not a band assignment.
4. At 48mm the midpoint seeds are B1 48, B2 96, B3 144, B4 192, B5 240.
5. If the requested band never exists before the board limit, return no canon match with the trace.
6. Ordered frame owns orientation: 3x4 and 4x3 are different canon records.
7. Square and rectangle are automatic canon. Diamond and triangle are explicit presets only.
8. The governed centre anchors the canon layout. Wrap may shift it only as far as legality requires.
9. No candidate magnet disappears without appearing in the recorded omissions.
10. `wrapGroup` stays whole-population-in, tightest-size-out. Nothing mutates the population after.
11. The released rim is 12mm. The 10mm floor is an admin bound, not a default.
12. Delete nothing until canon and the sweep have been seen side by side and agree.

## The build

### Task 0 — clear the debris from the mass-depth removal

- `units/classifier.ts` still says the frame is "not the legal bbox" and that a thin arm's sliver
  "seats nothing". Both are false at `cb2946e2`.
- The dead-limb counterexample hands the arm `masses: []` by hand. It never runs `safeSegments`, so
  it proves only that the reader ignores a segment you told it was empty. It passed before and after
  a change that inverted the behaviour it claims to guard.

**Done when:** the comment describes what the code does, and the test drives real segmentation. It
will then assert the OPPOSITE of what it says today — a 30mm arm's positions COUNT, because a magnet
centre genuinely sits in its 6mm band with full clearance either side.

### Task 1 — the lookup

Export the canon roster through the library door; it is not exported today, so nothing outside the
library can reach it. Then: pitch and ordered frame in, the one canon record out. No caller yet.

**Done when:** 3x4 returns portrait only, 4x3 landscape only, equal axes the square, never a diamond
or triangle, and a grid the board cannot hold returns nothing.

### Task 2 — the placer

Layout, shape and governed centre in; attempted, seated and omitted magnets out. No caller yet.

**Done when:** odd grids put a magnet on the centre and even grids put the gap there, outer edges and
holes reject seats, and every omission is visible in the result.

### Task 3 — the calibrator

Band in; this shape's own size interval and the grid at its midpoint out.

**Done when:** a square seeds at 144mm and stays band 3; a star seeded there grows until its material
genuinely reaches band 3; a shape that never reaches it returns nothing with the measurement.

### Task 4 — connect, additively

- One call that runs 1 to 12 with no browser.
- Canon becomes an ADDITIONAL candidate handed to the existing `wrapGroup`, beside the sweep.
- Band membership by measured legal extent — for canon AND for the sweep. The current test adds
  twice the rim to get an outer window; that is true only for a box. A star's real band-3 range is
  179-226mm and it is judged against 120-167, so every star answer is discarded today.
- The rim resolved once at the entry point, then passed explicitly.
- **Nothing is deleted in this task.** The worker's duplicate orchestration is removed in a separate
  step, after both sources have been seen side by side.

**Done when:** a no-browser call returns band calibration, ordered frame, anchor, canon identity,
omissions and wrapped size; canon is attempted first; the sweep still produces next-best; manual
controls and lattice are unchanged.

### Task 5 — prove it

Square, portrait rectangle, landscape rectangle, star, limbed shape, holed shape. Every fix defended
by a test that dies when the fix is reverted. Mutations that must fail: outer bbox replacing legal
extent; band floor or ceiling replacing the midpoint; a preset entering automatic match; the governed
anchor ignored; omission evidence deleted; wrap bypassed; coverage applied to the canon population;
canon routed through `registerLayout`.

Full suite, typecheck, scoped lint. Then the running bench: pick a band, see the canon answer first
with its size and what it dropped, next-best beneath it, lattice and manual controls untouched.

## Not in this

No scoring beyond canon-first. No garment 96mm lattice. No presets or D4 in automatic matching. No
new geometry, segmentation, centring or wrap. No new judge. Nothing deleted from manual controls,
lattice, coverage or the sweep.

## What changed from v1.1, and why

- **The classifier repair is gone.** Dan ruled the 16mm mass-depth an invented addition and it was
  removed at `cb2946e2`. It had cost the classifier one magnet position at every size and moved with
  a centring dial. With one depth, the band and the grid come from one reading.
- **The two-probe split is gone.** `legalUnionBoxMM` and a depth-compensated mass box are now the
  same box. One function, not two names for one extent.
- **The matcher is not replacing a live path.** The old family/fill matcher is dormant and has no
  production caller. Task 1 adds the first matching path that has ever existed.
- **Task 4 is additive, not a cutover.** v1.1 said make the worker transport, remove duplicate
  orchestration, leave no parallel automatic paths. That is the shape that unplugged manual pan, the
  belt and the lattice on 2026-08-29. Prove first, delete second.
- **Coverage comes off the canon path.** v1.1 applied it between seating and wrap, which would have
  returned a ring where the record says a full grid.
- **Task 0 is new** — debris from the mass-depth removal.

## Verdicts

**Necessity:** every existing working unit is reused; additions are limited to the four missing
connections plus the debris clearance.

**Sufficiency:** calibration, legal measurement, classification, centring, canon matching, anchored
seating, exact wrap, honest band membership and retained next-best are all delivered and proven.
